  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nWith weak high pressure across the Ohio Valley and a surface \ntrough across the High Plains, southeast surface winds have held\nsteady tonight across central Nebraska. However, across eastern\nportions of the state the winds are very light and dewpoint \ndepressions are only 1-4 degrees. This will set the stage for \nfog/low stratus through at least the early morning. We think \nthere will be just enough mixing to prevent widespread dense \nfog, but there could be some low spots with poor visibility. The\nRAP hydrolapse is favorable for fog across far eastern NE and \nwestern IA through sunrise.\n\nEarly morning convection is looking more likely early this\nmorning near or just northeast of the forecast - likely where \nSD/MN/IA come together. 1000-2000 MUCAPE and ~35kts of 0-6 bulk\nshear suggest the potential for a few strong to severe storms\nwith this early round of storms. The forcing along an advancing\ncold front is better north of the area this morning. The front\nis expected to make steady progress through the day, and we'll \nneed to watch for development back to the southwest along the \nboundary this afternoon. As it stands now, we keep most of the \nPoPs in western IA (15-30%), but far eastern NE is still in \nplay. Of note, we raised sky cover a bit (even after the morning\nstratus) because there will be a veil of smoke aloft today. \nThis can sometimes cause us to be 2-4 degrees too warm on high \ntemperatures, and it would also knock the suface-based \ninstability back just a bit.\n\nSaturday still looks to bring a better chance for more \nwidespread thunderstorms and also an increasing risk for severe \nweather with a damaging wind threat. Considerable model spread \nin both the global models and hi-res convective allowing models \nremain, but western IA looks more in higher confidence zone at \nthis point - especially for QPF totals. There is also a timing \nissue with convective initiation earlier in the day, perhaps \nwith a weakening trend through the peak heating hours, and then \nstrengthening with upscale growth as storms reach their\npotential once they cross the Missouri River in the late\nafternoon / early evening.\n\nAfter a couple warmer days on Sunday and Monday, the ridging\neases back and a more zonal flow returns with the upper jet\nsagging farther south across the northern CONUS. That'll be a\ncooler and relatively wet pattern. This means early signs of \noptimism for the drought stricken areas of Nebraska, Colorado, \nand Wyoming.",
